Date: Sun, 27 Jun 93 23:10:18 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Information"

One of the motivations for my interest in AIT is the possible light
it may shed on the question of what is and is not alive.  Traditional
definitions of life (that it eats, excretes, reproduces, metabolizes,
etc.) are a joke, IMO.  We need something more information-based.

As we move out into the universe, we may encounter strange new forms
of life.  Is it possible that sunspots are alive?  Perhaps other
metastable structures?  How about computer viruses?  Someone here
suggested some time back that "psychology" (the science itself) was alive. 
It would be nice to have a way, at least in principle, of deciding these
questions.   

One thing we observe is that life is complex - in some ways it is the 
most complex phenomenon we can find.  Yet we lack a good, objective
measure of complexity which is maximal for living creatures.

AIT, as Chaitin says in the article Tim posted, has the same failure that
entropy does as a measure.  Crystalline solids are minimally complex, and dis-
ordered gases are maximal.  Life falls somewhere in the middle.

But we can see that life is not simply partway between crystals and
gases in its complexity.  In an important sense, life is far more complex
than either of these.

The Physics of Computation book Tim recommended has an article comparing
various proposed complexity measures.  I forget what their results were
but I think this is still an important open issue.

This same question arises for Tim's example of an evolving Go strategy.
"Pure" AIT sees the end result of millions of games of Go evolution as
having no more information than a program based on a randomly chosen
string which plays senselessly.  Logical depth is perhaps a more appro-
priate measure of program complexity in this case.

Here is an interesting question.  Would there be expected to be a "natural"
and general complexity measure which is MAXIMAL for an OPTIMAL Go strategy?
No doubt the optimal Go strategy is very complex, probably beyond human
comprehension.  But perhaps to a transhuman, subtle and abstract symmetry
arguments will make it clear what the one best move is for any board
position.  This would be the optimal strategy.  Now, you could conceive of
a different strategy, one which is even more complex, but which is (by
definition) not as good.   

Or could you?  Is it possible that this not-as-good strategy, although 
having more SUPERFICIAL complexity, is actually not as complex in the
ways that matter (just as a gas is superficially more complex than a
cell composed of the same molecules)?  By "ways that matter" I mean not
just that the program wins the game, but rather that there is some
fundamental measure of complexity which naturally comes out maximal for
this optimal strategy.  This question is analogous to the one of whether
there is a natural complexity measure which is maximal for living things.

Hal

