Hal Finney has some more good ideas, including some relating to applying
AIT to studying life (and presumably A-LIFE). My posting of the Charles
Bennett paper on "Logical Depth and Physical Complexity" crossed with Hal's
message.

Hal writes:

>One of the motivations for my interest in AIT is the possible light
>it may shed on the question of what is and is not alive.  Traditional
>definitions of life (that it eats, excretes, reproduces, metabolizes,
>etc.) are a joke, IMO.  We need something more information-based.

I agree. The other definitions are too mechanistic, too "legalistic," and
end up missing the essence of what life is.

>
>As we move out into the universe, we may encounter strange new forms
>of life.  Is it possible that sunspots are alive?  Perhaps other
>metastable structures?  How about computer viruses?  Someone here
>suggested some time back that "psychology" (the science itself) was alive. 
>It would be nice to have a way, at least in principle, of deciding these
>questions.   

Recall Fred Hoyle's "The Black Cloud"? I was uneasy when I read it, some 25
years ago, because I couldn't see how a single such cloud could have had a
rich enough, an interesting engough, environment. Now, having gotten
familiar with complexity theory, the nature of evolution, the Anthropic
Principle (in its various forms), the results of Hayek and others on market
economies, and so on, I am fairly certain that we can place some bounds on
how complex an ecology/economy/"repro-ology" (my neologism again) needs to
be before life of some complexity emerges.

Living sunspots seems unlikely, if only because of the low information
transfer rates (I'm being vague here, so don't waste time on a flame).

>
>One thing we observe is that life is complex - in some ways it is the 
>most complex phenomenon we can find.  Yet we lack a good, objective
>measure of complexity which is maximal for living creatures.
>
>AIT, as Chaitin says in the article Tim posted, has the same failure that
>entropy does as a measure.  Crystalline solids are minimally complex, and dis-
>ordered gases are maximal.  Life falls somewhere in the middle.

As I always seem to be saying, be careful here. I some sense, a gas has
maximum complexity (maximum entropy, in another way of viewing it), but in
another sense it has nearly zero complexity, in that it is _so_ random, so
disordered, that it is best described only by a few scalar
qauntities...pressure, volume, temperature. (It is in this sense that we
think of a gas as having low complexity....what we mean is that the
organization of a gas, the structure, is of no meaning to us.)


>
>But we can see that life is not simply partway between crystals and
>gases in its complexity.  In an important sense, life is far more complex
>than either of these.
>
>The Physics of Computation book Tim recommended has an article comparing
>various proposed complexity measures.  I forget what their results were
>but I think this is still an important open issue.

I agree these remain open issues. And to me, of much more interest than the
"dreams of a final theory" that may or may not come from the Pork Collider.

(Anybody see the Economist cartoon? A picture of the huge tunnel being dug
for the SSC, with the caption: "Here's the hole, now pour in the money.")

But I digress.

>
>This same question arises for Tim's example of an evolving Go strategy.
>"Pure" AIT sees the end result of millions of games of Go evolution as
>having no more information than a program based on a randomly chosen
>string which plays senselessly.  Logical depth is perhaps a more appro-
>priate measure of program complexity in this case.

Well, Chaitin would agree. He thought about what Bennett later dubbed
"logical depth" some years back. And Wolfram called a similar idea
"computation irreducibility." This in the sense that a cellular automata.
to pick an example Wolfram has worked on a lot, has fairly low algorithmic
information content but very high "irreducibility" or logical
depth...meaning, you can't predict what's coming except by running the
computation. (BTW, this AIT-logical depth stuff has some important
implications for cryptography....simple shift registers may "look" random,
but are not, and one-time pads carry the maximum amount of
information--they are Chaitin's "results of random coin flips" strings--and
hence are not breakable.)

I share Hal's hopes--and those of Nick Szabo and Derek Zahn, I think--that
this stuff may give us a handle on the complexity of life and
self-reproducing systems.

(Like I said, I went to the 1987 first A-LIFE conference partly to hear
Chaitin speak on this very subject, but he cancelled out at the last
minute.)

And let me reiterate that I am not monomaniacal on this subject, not like
when I used to be on this list under my earlier Net name of Ken Tolman.

---pause-----

(This Sunday night joke brought to you via Klaus!)

I expect algorithmic information theory is just a small piece of things. As
I said earlier, there are all kinds of exciting things to study. Chaos,
space refining, pure math, health stuff, Prigogine, cellular automata,
nanotech shape compilers, mechanosynthesis, colloids and gels (as James
Donald just noted), and on and on.

After we exhaust AIT for the time being, in several weeks perhaps, we can
pick other topics. Or, clearly, anyone can propose their own seminar topics
at any time.

Best wishes, 

-Tim May

