Here are the first seven pages of Charles Bennett's paper on "Logical Depth
and Physical Complexity," taken from Herken's book. I didn't include the
more technical pages, which are interesting and which are certainly worth
reading, as they are just too technical and too hard to scan-in and OCR
(lots of equations with subscripts, etc.).

This stuff fits in with the "space-time" diagram of "program size-program
run time" diagram I posted a short while ago.(It also relates to logical
reversibility in some interesting ways.) Logical depth is closely related
to AIT--integrally related, in fact. And closely tied to issues of
"entropy" and "extropy" (the phrases "She's deep," and the cyberpunk
question, "Are you dense yet?" have resonances here).

Bennett also wrote a guest column on logical depth and Chaitin's Omega for
Martin Gardner's column, some years back. This article is very accessible
and is reprinted in Gardner's "Fractal Music, Hypercards and More...,"
Freeman, 1992. I don't have a copy of this book handy, else I'd've scanned
the article in. (Last night at a party, I was talking to "Forrest,"
formerly of our List, and telling him about the virtual seminar. He
surprised me by saying, "You know, there's another guy at IBM who had
similar ideas...really neat stuff." It turned out Forrest was thinking of
Bennett's article in "Sci. Am., which he remembered these many years later.
