literally--OCR errors may still remain, though I proofed it fairly
carefully and ran it through a spelling checker (which is more than I do
for my own posts!). Still, errors may remain.



[Source of this paper: Herken, Rolf, "The Universal Turing Machine: A
Half-Century Survey," Oxford University Press, 1988.]


Logical Depth and Physical Complexity

Charles H. Bennett

Abstract. Some mathematical and natural objects (a random sequence. a
sequence of zeros, a perfect crystal, a gas) are intuitively trivial, while
others (e.g., the human body, the digits of pi) contain internal evidence
of a nontrivial causal history.
    We formalize this distinction by defining an object's "logical depth"
as the time required by a standard universal Turing machine to generate it
from an input that is algorithmically random (i.e., Martin-Lof random).
This definition of depth is shown to be reasonably machine-independent, as
well as obeying a slow-growth law: deep objects cannot be quickly produced
from shallow ones by any deterministic process, nor with much probability
by a probabilistic process, but can be produced slowly.
    Next we apply depth to the physical problem of "self-organization",
inquiring in particular under what conditions (e.g., noise,
irreversibility, spatial and other symmetries of the initial conditions and
equations of motion) statistical-mechanical model systems can imitate
computers well enough to undergo unbounded increase of depth in the limit
of infinite space and time.

